Description: guard IPU6CameraData::workerThread completeBuffer() against
 requests whose pending set was cleared

Fixes a recurring wireplumber SIGABRT with this exact backtrace:

  FATAL default request.cpp:111 assertion "ret == 1" failed in completeBuffer()
    libcamera::Request::Private::completeBuffer(FrameBuffer*) +0x1fc
    libcamera::IPU6CameraData::workerThread() +0x176

The race path: the worker pops a Request from `pendingRequests_`,
releases `queueMutex_`, then spends ~50 ms copying HAL frame data into
the libcamera FrameBuffer. During that window a concurrent path clears
the request's `pending_` set. The worker then calls
`pipe()->completeBuffer(request, buffer)`, which asserts
`pending_.erase(buffer) == 1`; erase returns 0; abort.

Fix: verify the request still has pending buffers to complete before
calling completeBuffer; if not, drop the frame with a debug log.

Widened (2026-08-25, pkgrel=2) after seeing the guard fire in bursts
of 20+ per second during camera start/stop transitions:
  - Guard moved BEFORE the memcpy so stale requests skip the ~50 ms
    copy path (previously wasted ~1 s of CPU per burst).
  - Also check `request->status() == RequestPending` (a request in
    RequestComplete/RequestCancelled state should never be processed).
  - Log level downgraded from Warning to Debug (bursts are the
    expected behaviour of a working guard, not a warning-worthy event;
    at Warning they were flooding systemd-journald).

Extended (2026-08-26, pkgrel=3) after user reported the PipeWire graph
going stale after some uptime -- Firefox saw "target not found",
`systemctl --user restart pipewire ...` + Firefox restart made the
camera work again for a while, then it recurred. Sometimes a SIGSEGV
in `Request::Private::prepare()` (called from
`PipelineHandler::queueRequest()`) also fired, likely a downstream
memory-corruption knock-on.

Root cause: `PipelineHandler::completeRequest()` (pipeline_handler.cpp
line 592-600) is the only site that pops from
`Camera::Private::queuedRequests_`, and it stops at the first
`RequestPending` at the head:

    while (!data->queuedRequests_.empty()) {
        Request *req = data->queuedRequests_.front();
        if (req->status() == Request::RequestPending) break;
        ASSERT(!req->hasPendingBuffers());
        data->queuedRequests_.pop_front();
        camera->requestComplete(req);
    }

`Request` was pushed onto `queuedRequests_` in
`PipelineHandler::doQueueRequest()` (pipeline_handler.cpp:489) BEFORE
`queueRequestDevice()` handed it to IPU6's `pendingRequests_`. So
every request the worker skips has already been inserted into the
base `queuedRequests_`. If the guard silently skips (as pkgrel=1/2
did), that request stays there with status=RequestPending, and every
subsequent completion is blocked behind it. Which is exactly the
"PipeWire graph goes stale" symptom.

pkgrel=3 fix: in the skip path, when `status == RequestPending`, ALSO
call `pipe()->completeRequest(request)`. Safe because
`Request::Private::complete()` (request.cpp:128-140) asserts
`status==RequestPending` (yes) and `!hasPendingBuffers()` (yes, that's
why we skipped). If `pending_` was cleared by `doCancelRequest()`
(request.cpp:142-155) the `cancelled_` flag was already set (line
151), so `complete()` sets the terminal state to `RequestCancelled`;
otherwise `RequestComplete`. Both are terminal states the drain loop
pops. In the `status != RequestPending` subcase, keep pure-skip: a
second `complete()` would trip its own assertion.

This patch is applied via a Python string-replace in the PKGBUILD's
prepare(), NOT as a unified diff -- the change is a large multi-line
block replacement whose exact line offsets shifted after other patches
in the series, and string-replace is more robust than fighting with
patch's context-matching for this kind of edit. The Python code lives
in PKGBUILD::prepare(); this file is kept only for provenance (it
appears in `source=` so its sha256 is tracked, and its contents are
this human-readable explanation).

